  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Xeromphalina kauffmanii, commonly known as the Cross-veined Troop Mushroom, is a small woodland fungus characterized by its delicate fruiting bodies and distinctive gill structure. This species belongs to the Xeromphalinaceae family and is particularly noted for its appearance in the moist forest environments of New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cross-veined Gills",
      "description": "The gills are the most distinctive feature, displaying prominent cross-veins or ridges connecting the primary gills, creating a ladder-like or reticulate pattern",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size and Color",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies are typically small (1-3 cm across) with reddish-brown to orange-brown caps and pale, thin stems",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth in Troops",
      "description": "Mushrooms typically fruit in clusters or groups rather than singly, hence the common name 'Troop' mushroom",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Xeromphalina kauffmanii grows on decaying hardwood, moss-covered logs, and rich humus in cool, moist deciduous and mixed forests throughout New England, favoring shaded woodland areas.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The cross-veins on the gills of X. kauffmanii are thought to increase surface area for spore production and may help with water retention in the delicate fruiting structure.",
